Online services
---------------
The app talks to these, each under its own terms, which the MIT License does not cover. It sends them no analytics
and no telemetry, only the requests needed to show their data.
- TMDB (titles, pictures, where to stream, your list; api.themoviedb.org, image.tmdb.org):
  https://www.themoviedb.org/api-terms-of-use. This product uses the TMDB API but is not endorsed or certified by
  TMDB. The free API key is for non-commercial use; TMDB licenses commercial use separately.
- Open-Meteo (weather and the city search; api.open-meteo.com, geocoding-api.open-meteo.com):
  https://open-meteo.com/en/terms. The data is CC BY 4.0; the free API is for non-commercial use, and commercial use
  needs one of its plans.
- Wikidata (each title's id on the streaming services; www.wikidata.org): data under CC0,
  https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing.
- Binance (crypto prices; api.binance.com), only with show_crypto=true, which is off by default:
  https://www.binance.com/en/terms.

"Watch on..." and trailers open the title in its own app, through that service's link (Netflix, Prime Video,
Disney+, Apple TV, Max, YouTube). The app sends nothing to those services itself; the opened app follows its own terms.

